---
name: decide_context
description: >
  The always-on context block appended to a live decide observation (issue
  #580): the sim clock time, the plan's current stop, and how long the agent
  has been on that stop. Rendered by cognition.decide_context_block() only
  when the step loop threads a SimClock (the bake and the offline tests pass
  none). The deterministic mock brain reads only the observation's first
  line, so this block can never change a mock decision. The numbers are
  computed in Python (they are data, not text); only the wording lives here.
inputs:
  time:
    type: string
    description: In-game wall-clock time, e.g. "Monday 12:05 PM".
  place:
    type: string
    description: The current stop's place, e.g. "Houston Hall".
  activity:
    type: string
    description: The current stop's activity, e.g. "eating lunch".
  minutes:
    type: integer
    description: >
      The stop's intended duration in minutes. Optional; the planned clause
      is dropped when unset (the schedule says stay indefinitely) or 0.
  elapsed:
    type: integer
    description: >
      Minutes this stop has been the agent's current one, re-anchored on
      arrival at the stop's own place so the walk there is excluded (leaving
      it commensurate with the planned minutes). NOT "time spent at the
      place": an agent that wanders off-plan never arrives, so the clock
      keeps running on the stop it is neglecting -- which is the point
      (#826). Hence "this has been your current stop", not "you have been
      here", which would be false for exactly that agent. The sentence is
      dropped at 0 -- the stop just became current.
  finished:
    type: boolean
    description: >
      Is this stop already done, with its pointer held back by the next stop's
      start_hour (#838)? The loop credits a completed activity wherever it ran
      (#831) but will not run the plan ahead of an anchored hour, so a finished
      errand can stay "current" for up to an hour. Saying so REPLACES the
      elapsed clause: in #760 batch 5 an agent read "77 min" on a planned-15
      coffee break it had already had, called itself behind schedule, and walked
      62 sim-minutes back across campus to redo it.
  next_place:
    type: string
    description: The next stop's place, e.g. "Library". Omitted at the last stop.
  next_activity:
    type: string
    description: The next stop's activity, e.g. "meeting a friend".
  next_hour:
    type: string
    description: >
      The next stop's start_hour in words, e.g. "10 AM". Rendered only when that
      hour is still ahead -- see next_due.
  next_in:
    type: integer
    description: >
      Whole minutes from now until next_hour. Given explicitly so the model does
      not have to subtract two clock times to learn whether a walk fits.
  next_due:
    type: boolean
    description: >
      Has the anchor hour already arrived? True renders "due now" instead of a
      start time. No in-loop render ever sets this true: any agent that
      reaches a decide already had `advance()` attempted the same tick, and
      its refusal implies the anchor is still ahead. This is defence in depth
      for a #366 straggler decide thread building its observation from a
      stale snapshot while the schedule is mutated underneath it, not the
      common case -- kept because printing "starting at 10 AM" at 10:05 would
      be the #812 false-time-word class if that path is ever hit.
  day_end:
    type: string
    description: >
      When the day ends, in clock words, e.g. "8 PM" (#891). Rendered -- with
      day_end_in -- only inside the run's final stretch, where it changes
      decisions: batch 8 had an agent start a ~50-minute walk 23 minutes
      before a day end nothing had ever told it about. Omitted earlier in the
      day and whenever the loop threads no day length (the bake, offline
      tests).
  day_end_in:
    type: integer
    description: >
      Whole minutes from now until day_end. Explicit for the same reason as
      next_in -- the model should not have to do clock arithmetic to learn
      whether a walk fits in the day.
  held:
    type: integer
    description: >
      Minutes the agent's current PERFORMED activity text has gone unchanged
      (#905). Set only past cognition.SAME_ACT_CONTEXT_MIN, so a normal study
      block is never nagged. Distinct from elapsed, which clocks the schedule
      stop: a re-chosen off-plan wait ("reviewing problems while waiting for
      theo", 340 min in #760 batch 10) never shows up in elapsed at all. The
      sentence is deliberately factual with no directive -- the model decides
      whether the hold is still worth it.
  held_activity:
    type: string
    description: The unchanged activity text itself, quoted back verbatim.
  absent:
    type: string
    description: >
      First name of a cast member held_activity mentions who is NOT here
      (#905): a person-contingent wait decays with every minute the person
      doesn't show, but their absence is otherwise only ever the absence of
      an observation. Omitted when the activity names no absent person.
sample:
  time: Monday 12:05 PM
  place: Houston Hall
  activity: eating lunch
  minutes: 40
  elapsed: 15
  finished: false
---
Right now it is {{ time }}.
Your plan's current stop: {{ activity }} at {{ place }}{% if minutes %} (planned ~{{ minutes }} min){% endif %}.{% if finished %} You have already finished this stop.{% if next_place %} Your next stop is {{ next_activity }} at {{ next_place }}{% if next_due %}, due now.{% else %}, starting at {{ next_hour }} ({{ next_in }} min from now).{% endif %}{% endif %}{% elif elapsed %} This has been your current stop for {{ elapsed }} min.{% endif %}{% if held %} You have been doing exactly this -- "{{ held_activity }}" -- for {{ held }} min{% if absent %}, and {{ absent }} is not here{% endif %}.{% endif %}{% if day_end %} The day ends at {{ day_end }} ({{ day_end_in }} min from now).{% endif %}
